Malcolm Ryan wrote:

> On Mar 6, 12:12 am, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
> wrote:
>> The problem isn't that there are too many classes, the problem is that
>> they aren't sufficiently differentiated.  Removing some (for whatever
>> reason) doesn't really solve the problems in vanilla.
> 
> I'm curious to know why lack of differentiation is a 'problem' that
> needs to be fixed.

Speaking as a designer, lack of differentiation means that the design
failed. If the players manage to have fun with the little variation there
is, that is a lucky incidence. But providing a set of options should mean
that they are important. 

You will say that having different classes, quests, gods etc. provides the
variety. But since gameplay is often similar (the early game is good, as
stated to death over here :), this type of variation only reaches player
who have an eye for flavour (role-players. if you want). For the others
(puzzlers, gamblers, minmaxers), it's moot.

David

PS: I apologise, but I'll mention it anyway: Crawl boasts 25 races and 28
classes. No matter what people say, these are not completely well
differentiated. We found certain overlaps so bad that two races got just
axed. (Nethack has fewer entries in both counts, so drastic steps are less
needed.)


